layout: post
title:  "Neon Dragons"
date:   2025-10-20 21:47:03 -0700
categories: ttrpgs
---

Neon Dragons is a role-playing game about being a lowlife semi-superhuman enhanced by drugs and access to weapons running amok in neon-drenched downtown. Semi-Superhuman is a confusing term-- it essentially means that you won't be flying or flipping cars anytime soon, but things like slowed perception of time or miraculously dodging a bullet are completely in your wheelhouse.

Think of yourself or your character as about the same power level as The Punisher, or Daredevil. Getting shot will typically kill you, but you're a step up from the normal person.

As this character (A Dragon, if you would), you are beholden only to your own code of ethics. You could spend your time doing anything from killing off members of the mob who are trying to kill your friends and family, or working for your local Yakuza, or participating in riots against the police. Think of this as Cyberpunk, but without the cyber. The modern, chemically-altered Youxia.

Unlike many games, Neon Dragons does not come with a real built-in progression system. At least, not mechanically. It's the GMs discretion at what point Dragons are allowed to gain new upgrades or new weapons (well... exceptions apply. Check out the Weapons section below). Surviving a lot doesn't necessarily mean you will survive more. This game is meant to be played with an ideal of one GM and 1-6 players, either as a one-shot or until all Dragons (or all Dragons but one) are dead. But don't let me tell you what to do.
You may die. That's why it's so easy to make new Dragons.
Main inspirations include:

	* Katana: ZERO (Askiisoft)
	* Daredevil (Marvel)
	* The Punisher (Marvel)
	* CAIN (Tom Bloom/Abaddon)
	* Hotline Miami (Askiisoft)
	* My Friend Pedro (Askiisoft)

## **DRAGONS**
If you are hit, you die. Dragons may move a reasonable amount on their turn, then take an action, which is typically attacking but could be anything else that takes about the same amount of time and/or effort.

Enemies can either be close to you, or they can be far. Close means within hand's reach, far means any further than that. Dragons can only attack enemies close to them.

In order to hit a target, roll 1d6 (a Hit Roll). If the result is 4+, you hit and deal 1 damage to your target. This is enough to kill most people. On anything less, your target counterattacks, rolling 1d6 and hitting (possibly killing) on a 5+.

Each time you kill, you gain 1 Adrenaline. For each point of Adrenaline you have, you may choose an additional target for attacks or Item usage. When you attack multiple targets, roll a single attack roll and apply it to each target. You can have a maximum of 6 Adrenaline, which is reset at the end of each fight. You can spend 1 Adrenaline after an attack to take another action. You may also spend 1 Adrenaline after being hit to ignore it.

When your character tries to accomplish something outside of combat, roll 1d6. On a 3+, succeed. If the action is hard, you need a 6 to succeed instead. For each that is true, roll another 1d6:

* Someone is helping you
* You have an advantage
* You are skilled at the task.

Then choose the highest roll.

`Each character may choose two skills when they are created. These are one-word descriptors of things you are generally good at. Skills must be specific, like Climbing, Gambling, or Lying-- vague concepts like "Everything" are not allowed.

### *Aesthetic*
Dragons are typically focused on a single aesthetic-- whether this is because of the brain damage sustained from the various chronotropics, analgesics, and amphetamines they take or simply a decision based on looking dripped the fuck out tends to depend on the individual Dragon. There's no mechanical requirement for an aesthetic, but it's recommended to take one anyways. Because it's cool as hell. Aesthetics are typically one word and should probably stay away from "-core" or "-punk".
Examples might be:

* Goth
* Samurai
* Neon
* Black Tie
* Youxia
* Construction
* Bubblegum

But by the gold in the temple, don't just choose from the list. Think about it. Make up some of your own.

## **WEAPONS**

Your signature, your mark upon the battlefield. Every Dragon chooses one at character creation. Each weapon has three upgrades, affecting their uses both in and out of a fight. Upgrades are unlocked whenever your GM decides it's appropriate, or, if everyone at the table agrees, whenever the party hits the Club (see The Club)

`Unless otherwise stated, Dragons can only attack Near enemies.`

**KATANA**

Whenever you would be hit, roll 1d6 (a Deflect Roll). On a 6, the damage is reduced to 0.

_Upgrades:_
* Sublime Parry: Deflect rolls succeed on 5s and 6s.
* Ballistic Physics: Successful deflect rolls on ranged attacks kill a far enemy.
* Storm of Blades: When you would attack multiple targets, attack one more.

[Could also be: Machete, Jian, Rapier]

**BASEBALL BAT**

Whenever you kill an enemy, make an attack against another enemy at far range (Homerun Attack)

_Upgrades:_
* Deadeye: If your homerun attack also kills an enemy, make another attack against another far enemy.
* Here to Louisville: Attacks against enemies that don't kill Daze instead. Dazed enemies can't act until the end of their next turn.
* Momentum: If you moved before attacking and you would gain Adrenaline this turn, gain another point of Adrenaline.

[Could also be: Throwing knives, Bow and Arrow, Rifle]

**CLEAVER**

You may throw the Cleaver as an action, making an attack against an enemy at far range. You can't use the Cleaver until you retrieve the Cleaver as an action. If your Cleaver doesn't kill after throwing it, it is instead stuck in your target, giving them -1 on all rolls (they must roll a 6+ to hit). You do need to move close to your hatchet to retrieve it.

_Upgrades:_
* Perfect Throw: Instead of you or an ally taking damage, you may throw the cleaver to intercept the attack, causing it to deal 0 damage.
* Slaughterhouse: You may immediately move close to your target and retrieve the cleaver after throwing it.
* Repeat Offender: If you miss an attack against any target, you may spend 1 Adrenaline to reroll your attack against that target. This does not count as a separate attack.

[Could also be: Hatchet, Pistol, Hammer]

**MEATHOOK**

On a hit, drag another target from far range to close range.

_Upgrades:_
* Two-Piece: When you drag a target from far range to close range, another character may immediately attack them.
* Mine Now: On a kill, you may also take your target's weapon (if they have one).
* Spin to Win: When you would attack multiple targets, instead attack all near enemies (roll once and apply it to all near enemies)

[Could also be: Kusarigama, Chains, Noose]

**FISTS**

You start combat with two Flurry points. You may expend a Flurry point when you attack to roll another die and take whichever one is higher. You regain a Flurry point when you land an Overkill.

_Upgrades:_
* Take Hostage: You may expend a Flurry point to take a near target hostage. Any damage you would take they take instead. Effect ends when they die.
* One-Two: On an attack roll of 6, make another attack against a near target.
* Qi Flow: At the start of any turn where you have no Flurry points, gain 1 Adrenaline.

[Could also be: Staff, Switchblade, Butterfly Knives]

**SLEDGEHAMMER**

Whenever you attack a character and miss, you may choose to knock them to far range.

_Upgrades:_
* Fore!: When you hit with this weapon, you may knock your target into another target at Far range, allowing you to make an attack against them.
* Pile Driver: Before you attack, you may spend any amount of Adrenaline and add it to your Hit Roll. For each number above 6 that you roll, kill another near enemy.
* Blood Rush: Whenever you Overkill an enemy, gain 1 Adrenaline and you may immediately move.

[Could also be: Fire Axe, Stop Sign, Medieval Mace]

## **DRUGS**
Now we're cooking with gas. Dragons have a Cocktail, a specially handcrafted mix of performance enhancing substances from pre-existing medications and items (dubbed Liquors). Dragons have two Doses of their cocktail and recover all Doses whenever they hit the Club (see the section after this one). You may inject or ingest it a dose before a fight, before anyone acts, or on your turn, which takes no action. You may only ingest one cocktail per fight. You can ingest more, but you immediately die after the battle ends, either from brain damage, heart failure, or liver explosion. A cocktail can have a maximum of three Liquors in their cocktail, and the effects of Liquors stack. Effects end at the end of a fight.
[Note to the narrative-minded: it may be fun to describe the injector or medium through which your character takes the Cocktail.]

- _WHITE:_ Whenever you would gain Adrenaline, gain 1 more (+1 per stack).

- _RED:_ All attacks you make have +1 on the Hit Roll (+1 per stack).

- _ORANGE:_ Whenever you would be hit, you may make a Dodge roll (1d6). On a 4+, you dodge the attack. All effects and damage are negated. For every stack, the number needed is reduced by 1 (3+, 2+, etc.)

- _YELLOW:_ You may reroll 1s. You may choose either total. You may only do this once per roll. For every stack, the threshold for a reroll increases. (Reroll 1s and 2s, reroll 1s and 2s and 3s, etc.)

- _GREEN:_ The first time you would take damage, ignore it. For every stack, the number of times you would take damage increases by 1 (the first and second time, the first and second and third time, etc.)

- _BLUE:_ Whenever you roll a 6 on a hit roll, you may take another action. For every stack, the threshold increases by 1 (whenever you roll a 5 or 6, whenever you roll a 4, 5, or 6, etc.).

- _PURPLE:_ Whenever an enemy counterattacks, you may roll 1d6 and deal a hit to them on a 6+. For every stack, the threshold increases by 1 (5+, 4+, etc.)

- _BLACK:_ At the start of any turn where you have no Adrenaline, gain 1 Adrenaline. (+1 Adrenaline per stack)

## **THE CLUB**
Everybody's gotta lay back once in a while. The Club is a safe place for most Dragons, hidden in a back alley or wild district far from the guns and batons of the cops, Yakuza, or whoever else your enemies may be. The Dragons hit The Club after accomplishing some sort of dangerous goal with several fight scenes or to split up long sessions of intense violence with (comparatively) wholesome downtime.

After entering The Club, Dragons regain all doses of their Cocktail and may swap out any liquors in it for any other liquors.

`Note to the narrative minded: it may be fun to describe where you're getting these doses from or how you're making them.`

Other than that, The Club is a safe place to hide out for a while as the heat dies down outside and the Dragons to rest and recuperate. Play cards. Do slice-of-life things. This is a chance to show how your Dragon operates when *not* cutting apart dozens of hapless mooks.

### *GM's Note*
If there are any important plot hooks, put them here. If there are any news broadcasts your players should see, put them here. If there are any important NPCs to be met with that you *don't* want to be killed, put them here. Of course, nothing I can do about it if you don't, but hey! Might be a good idea.

Additionally, The Club is only called "The Club" because of its flavorful intention-- this could easily be a hideout, penthouse apartment, or nuclear bomb shelter.

As an optional rule (that is, only to be integrated if everyone at the table agrees), each time the Dragons return to The Club and recover, they gain a new weapon upgrade or skill.

`And as a tiny little secret down here, if you really want to raise the stakes, try putting The Club in danger. Don't do this too often. I recommend no more than twice a campaign, really. It gets old quick, I promise.`

## **ENEMIES**
Most enemies die in one hit. If an enemy is killed by a dice that rolled a 6, it's considered an Overkill and you steal an Item from them. Items can be used once on your turn, taking no action, then are discarded. If you would take multiple items, choose any one. You can only be carrying one item at a time. Items persist through fights.

Enemies will be cut down in the dozens with each attack-- they should vastly outnumber the Dragons. To keep things simple, these rules recommend only having a maximum of three or four types of enemies in a single fight. These rules, of course, can't force you to do anything.

`Enemies do not take turns and instead react to the actions of the Dragons. Describe what the enemies do after each player takes their movement and action.

**GUNMAN**
* Bang!: Whenever another enemy counterattacks, a gunman may also respond. This counts as a second counterattack.

_Pistol_ (ITEM): Make an attack against a character at far range.

**THUG**
* Overwhelm: If 3 thugs or more are Close to a target, that target can't move or gain adrenaline.

_Switchblade_ (ITEM): Make an attack against a character at close range.

**SHOTGUNNER**
* Buckshot: Damage from the Shotgunner can't be reduced or negated by any effects.

_Shotgun_ (ITEM): Make an attack against 2 enemies at far range (roll once, apply the result to both).

**RIOT COP**
* Sturdy: The first time the Riot Cop would be hit, ignore it. Overkill bypasses this ability.

_Shield_ (ITEM): When you are hit, discard this item to ignore the damage

**SKINNY**
* Quick: The Skinny makes an attack against its attacker before its attacker does. 

_Crowbar_ (ITEM): Kill an enemy at far range.

**LEADER
* Orders: Allies of the Leader have +1 to all rolls. Whenever the Leader would be killed, it may instead sacrifice a near ally to ignore the attack.

_Ornate Revolver_ (ITEM): Make an attack against a Close or Far enemy. If it kills, gain 1 Adrenaline.

**JUNKIE**
* Addict: Under the effects of one liquor.

_Emergency Dose_ (ITEM): Acts as an additional dose of your cocktail.

**GRENADIER**
* Boom! Boom! Boom!: Attacks a far character. Apply the same roll to all other characters (Dragons and enemies) close to them.

_Molotov_ (ITEM): Throw to kill a far enemy and make a hit roll against all characters (Dragons and enemies) close to them (roll once and apply the same roll). 

### *KNIGHTS*
While Dragons are indeed a cut above the normal person, there are a certain sect of people that have dedicated themselves to killing them, whether through government training, oaths of vengeance, or good ol' fashioned fun. These people are known as Knights. Most are typically private military groups taking hold of a rare opportunity to take down the Dragons that replaced them and make bank doing it, but really, any amount of skill could make you a knight if you have the dedication.

If you'd like to show off a particular powerful enemy (or "miniboss", if the vernacular suits you better), attach the Knight template to them. The first time a Knight enemy is hit, they may ignore it. Knights also have turns in addition to reacting to Dragon actions as normal, where they may make a hit roll against any near or far character. Knights start their turn before any Dragons act.

` Knights that are part of a unified group can make for great campaign enemies! Give them an aesthetic to follow and a cool punchy name in all caps that's probably just two words slapped together. Examples might be:
	- `BLACKSTAR
	- `DEADHEAD
	- `YGGDRASIL
	- `SERAPHIM
	- `GOLDWIND
	- `HIGHBROW

## **BOSSES**

Bosses take multiple hits to kill. They have multiple abilities as well.

**SNIPE**

_3 Hits_
* Crosshair: At the start of a fight, choose a target to Aim at. If they're still within sight of you at the end of your turn, they take 1 damage.
* Smoke Bomb: When someone gets Near you, you may immediately Daze them (dazed enemies can't act until the end of their next turn) and move until you are Far from all enemies.

**BRUTE**

_5 Hits_
* Punish: Whenever a near enemy rolls a 1, you may immediately make an attack against them.
* Pounds of Meat: You are only damaged by a 5+ on the Hit Roll instead of 4+.

**FIEND**

_2 Hits_
* Adversary: You have a cocktail you imbibe at the beginning of combat. It may have up to 2 liquors.
* Duelist: You may choose any weapon from the weapon list to use as your own. It has no upgrades.
